---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SubscribeForm from '../components/SubscribeForm.astro';
import { CADENCE_LINE } from '../lib/site';

// THE SUBSCRIBE DOOR (editors' desktop review, 2026-09-07).
//
// WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. The email field stood in the footer of every page on the
// site, one element among four rows of links, and the editors replaced it there
// with a link reading SUBSCRIBE TO THE JOURNAL. It reads SUBSCRIBE — IT’S FREE
// now, reworded twice on 2026-09-12; same target, same place each time. The
// instruction behind the original move was explicit
// that the form move rather than go: a journal that stops offering the door
// while keeping the sign for it has done the one thing this change was not for.
// So the field is here, and it is the whole of the page — a reader who presses
// that link arrives at the thing they pressed it for, above the fold at every
// width, with nothing to scroll past to reach it.
//
// THE CADENCE COMES FROM CADENCE_LINE, never from a sentence typed here. It is
// the journal's own present-tense statement of how often it publishes (R-039,
// R-055), it renders on the masthead of every issue and in the digest, and it
// moves when cadence moves. A page that told a prospective subscriber how often
// mail would arrive, in its own words, would be the one surface that went stale
// the day the cadence changed.
//
// NO PROMISES UNDER THE FIELD. "No tracking, unsubscribe anytime." was retired
// on 2026-09-02 — the practice unchanged, the claim moved to the terms, where it
// binds. This page does not reinstate it in different words, in a new place,
// three weeks later; it links to the terms instead, which is where a reader can
// hold us to something. The receipt a reader sees after pressing Subscribe still
// names the unsubscribe link, because at that point it is a fact about the mail
// they are about to get rather than reassurance offered to win the press.
//
// THAT RULE STANDS, AND THE LINE BETWEEN THE TWO KINDS OF CLAIM IS NOW RULED
// — both editors, 2026-09-12. A present-tense description of the visible form
// may appear above the field; a promise of future conduct lives in the terms
// and never on this page. "Just your email. That's all we ask." is the first
// kind. The retired line was the second.
//
// THE ABOVE-THE-FIELD SPACE IS FULL. That one sentence is the last; any further
// addition there, of either kind, needs a dual editor yes recorded before the
// PR is opened. The markup comment beside the line carries the reasoning, which
// is worth reading before proposing the exception that seems obviously fine.
//
// A SIGNUP IS A SUBSCRIPTION, since 2026-08-22 — there is no confirmation step
// to describe, and this page must not invent one. See src/lib/subscribe-copy.mjs,
// which holds the words for what actually happens next.
---

<Base
  title="Subscribe"
  description="Each issue of The Latent Review, delivered by email when it publishes."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="kicker kicker--accent kicker--lead">Subscribe</p>
      <h1>Receive each issue when it publishes</h1>
      <p class="page-note">{CADENCE_LINE}</p>
      {/*
        WHAT THIS SENTENCE IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE RETIRED ONE — RATIFIED BY
        BOTH EDITORS, 2026-09-12. A present-tense description of the visible
        form may appear above the field; a promise of future conduct lives in
        the terms and never on this page. This sentence is the first kind: one
        field, nothing else asked at signup, checkable by looking down the page.
        The line retired on 2026-09-02 was the second kind, and a promise is the
        kind of claim that needs the terms to bind it.

        AND THIS IS THE LAST SENTENCE ABOVE THE FIELD — the ceiling is part of
        the same ratification, and is the whole reason the distinction above is
        safe to write down. ANY FURTHER ADDITION HERE, OF EITHER KIND, REQUIRES
        A DUAL EDITOR YES RECORDED BEFORE THE PR IS OPENED. Not argued for in
        the PR; recorded first.

        WHY THE CEILING EXISTS, so that a session meeting it does not read it as
        fussiness. The distinction above is defensible, which is exactly its
        danger: it is a reading that justifies one line, and it would justify a
        second and a third in the same words, each time by a session acting in
        good faith. A page reaches four lines of reassurance above its field
        without anyone ever deciding to have four. The ceiling is what makes the
        distinction a rule rather than a lever.

        IT IS A CLAIM ABOUT THE FORM, SO IT MOVES WITH THE FORM. If the field
        ever gains a companion — a name, a preference, anything — this sentence
        is false the moment that ships, and nothing in the build will say so.
        Docketed on the pricing-review item in docs/BACKLOG.md, beside the
        footer label, for exactly that reason.
      */}
      <p class="page-note">Just your email. That’s all we ask.</p>
    </header>

    <SubscribeForm />

    <div class="prose subscribe-page-note">
      {/*
        THE CADENCE-AND-TERMS LINE IS DELETED (editors, 2026-09-07). It read
        "One email an issue. What we do with the address is set out in the
        terms." — a frequency the dateline above already states through
        CADENCE_LINE, and a pointer to a page the footer links from every page
        of the site. Removed rather than reworded: the field is what this page
        is for, and a note under it that repeats the page above it and the
        footer below it is a third statement of two facts.
      */}
      <p>
        Reading us by machine instead? The <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/feed.json">JSON</a> feeds carry every piece, and{' '}
        <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a> maps the site.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The note sits under the field rather than above it, so nothing stands
     between a reader who arrived to subscribe and the field they came for. */
  .subscribe-page-note {
    max-width: 30rem;
    margin: 2.6rem auto 0;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* The `p + p` rule that stood here spaced two paragraphs; there is one now,
     and it is deleted rather than left to style a sibling that no longer
     exists. The block's own top margin is unchanged, so the line sits exactly
     where the pair used to begin — no gap where the first one was. */
</style>
